7592186045417] [17592186065268 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065269 #edb/ref 1061 72737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065269 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "222d87d8-a41a-5329-ad4b-a1a65afd817d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065269 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065340 #edb/ref 1064 "To factor a polynomial expression like $2a^2+6a$, we follow the three steps below:\n\n1. First, we find the greatest common factor (or $\\textrm{GCF}$) of both terms. In this case,\n$\\textrm{GCF}(2a^2,6a) = 2a$.\n2. Then, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n$2a^{2} + 6a|=; (2a \\cdot a) + (2a \\cdot 3)$\n3. Finally, we write the $\\textrm{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n$(2a \\cdot a) + (2a \\cdot 3)|=; 2a(a + 3)$\n\nTherefore, the factored form of $2a^2+6a$ is $2a(a+3)$.\n\nWe can check that we factored correctly by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a(a + 3) &=  \\\\\n2a \\cdot a + 2a \\cdot 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 2a^{2} + 6a \\mid ✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065340 #edb/ref 1065 1595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065340 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f84c09f1-b24a-50f1-a8c4-24b8d857bca2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065340 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065414 #edb/ref 1064 "Equations can contain more than one variable. For example, the following equation has two variables, $\\color{blue}x$ and ${\\color{red}y}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x} - 7{\\color{red}y} = 1\n$$\n\nTo **solve the equation for the variable ** ${\\color{blue}{x}},$ we need to isolate $\\color{blue}x$ on one side of the equation:  \n\nIn this case, we apply the addition principle and add $7{\\color{red}y}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-7y & =1 \\\\ x-7y+7y & =1+7y \\\\ x+0 & =1+7y \\\\ x & =1+7y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully isolated the variable ${\\color{blue}{x}}.$\n\nLet's now look at an example where we must apply the multiplication principle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065414 #edb/ref 1065 8072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065414 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c431a781-752f-59ae-bf37-616b43771b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065414 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186482574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068701 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068486 #edb/ref 175